from record import source as S, edge as E, replace as R, record as P

p='kathleen_wilson';s='tq234_wilson_intro2004'
P(p,
'A historian of Britain and its eighteenth-century empire. In her introduction to A New Imperial History, Wilson argues that imperial encounters helped constitute identities within Britain as well as overseas. Difference is an instrument of power, while identities are historically imposed and negotiated through legal status, gender, slavery and performance. She uses Chakrabarty’s challenge to Europe as history’s universal subject and Catherine Hall’s insistence on theorizing difference, without proposing that cultural analysis replace political or social history. Her account of colonial governmentality draws explicitly on Foucault. The later Strolling Players of Empire continues the inquiry through performance and colonial rule. Her work therefore connects cultural and political explanation; it does not treat every imperial setting as identical or empire as a one-way export from a metropolitan centre.',
'Primary introduction (2004), selected pp. 1–8 with nn. 3, 6 and 17, read from Cambridge’s authorized excerpt. First-person institutional statement and CV also checked. Full introduction beyond the excerpt and complete books unread. Named resources support specific uses, not exclusive intellectual descent. Original Island Race 2003 remains distinct from reprints. No QID invented; no Indigenous narrative or traditional knowledge retained.',
sources=[S(s,'Kathleen Wilson, “Introduction: Histories, Empires, Modernities,” A New Imperial History (2004), authorized excerpt.','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805210/07962/excerpt/9780521007962_excerpt.pdf','Selected printed pp. 1–8, especially 2/n3, 3/n6, 4–6 and 7/n17 read. Colonial difference, coerced identity and metropolitan/colonial relations; sources’ originals and rest of the introduction unread.')],
edges=[E(p,'chakrabarty','dipesh_chakrabarty',p,'Wilson uses Chakrabarty’s critique of Europe as history’s universal subject to frame the limits of conventional imperial narratives.',s,'Primary introduction p. 2/n3, body argument rather than bibliography alone. A specific methodological resource; Provincializing Europe is not newly read.'),E(p,'hall','catherine_hall',p,'Wilson builds on Hall’s call to theorize difference in writing imperial history.',s,'Primary p. 3/n6, explicitly quoted and used in the framing argument. Culture does not displace political/social histories; Wilson’s qualification remains.'),E(p,'governmentality','foucault',p,'Wilson employs governmentality to examine colonial interventions in subjects’ social and gendered lives.',s,'Primary p. 7/n17 explicitly links the historical argument to Foucault’s Governmentality. Bounded conceptual use, not full theoretical allegiance.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'post','Examines how colonial difference and imperial encounters constituted identities within Britain as well as overseas.',s,'Primary introduction pp. 2–6, joining identity and power while rejecting a replacement orthodoxy.'),R(p,3,p,'nationalismstudies','Studies national belonging as a historical product of imperial power, difference and negotiated identities.',s,'Primary pp. 3–6, not merely the future project on the faculty page. No timeless national character or nationalist political allegiance inferred.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='pp. 1–8, selected body and specified notes',access='Cambridge publisher excerpt; no community content retained')],holds=['Remaining introduction and complete monographs unread; cited source originals not newly read.'])

p='sheila_fitzpatrick';s='tq234_fitzpatrick_ludtke2008';r='tq234_fitzpatrick_revision2008'
P(p,
'A historian of Soviet social life and Stalinism. Fitzpatrick’s retrospective describes revisionism as a challenge to political-science domination and the totalitarian model, while retaining divisions among revisionists and between their generations. Her research follows mobility, everyday practices and people’s encounters with state power rather than treating society as a passive object of rule. In a comparative chapter coauthored with Alf Lüdtke, she takes Arendt’s atomization hypothesis as a question for empirical investigation: relationships could be broken and reconfigured, and participation also required attention to people’s emotional investments. The argument preserves Arendt’s recognition of support from below while questioning whether atomization adequately describes the societies concerned. Everyday Stalinism and the later political-team study connect social and political history; detailed everyday research alone does not establish a microhistory affiliation.',
'Primary coauthored opening extract of “Energizing the Everyday” read, not the full pp. 266–301 chapter. Fitzpatrick’s 2008 retrospective abstract and displayed authorial notes read, not the complete article. Sydney/ACU institutional accounts retain their mediated status. Alf Lüdtke receives equal chapter credit; Arendt’s original not newly read. Selected books unread in full; microhistory affiliation remains held.',
sources=[S(s,'Sheila Fitzpatrick and Alf Lüdtke, “Energizing the Everyday,” Beyond Totalitarianism (2008), pp. 266–301.','https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511802652.008','Primary opening extract, all six displayed paragraphs, read. Jointly questions the empirical fit of Arendt’s atomization hypothesis, retaining her account of support from below and asking about emotional self-energizing. Full chapter unread.'),S(r,'Sheila Fitzpatrick, “Revisionism in Retrospect: A Personal View,” Slavic Review 67:3 (2008): 682–704.','https://doi.org/10.2307/27652945','Author abstract and displayed notes 1, 5 read: retrospective account, internal divisions and limits of remembered ideological affiliations. Full article unavailable here; Cambridge online date 2017 is not original 2008.')],
edges=[E(p,'arendt',p,'hannah_arendt','With Alf Lüdtke, questions whether atomization adequately explains everyday relationships and participation under Stalinism and Nazism.',s,'Joint primary opening extract, paras. 1–6. Retains Arendt’s attention to support from below and proposes investigation of broken/reconfigured social bonds; no claim of reading the complete chapter or Arendt’s original.',kind='critique')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'social','With Alf Lüdtke, investigates the breaking and reconfiguration of everyday social bonds under Stalinism and Nazism.',s,'Joint primary opening extract, paras. 1–6; social relations and emotional investments, not merely an institutional field label.'),R(p,2,p,'political','Connects domination with participation from below and the emotional investments of everyday political life.',s,'Joint primary extract on Arendt and the internal dynamics of regimes. Describing participation does not deny coercion or confer equal power.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Complete displayed opening extract; chapter pp. 266–301 not fully read'),dict(source_id=r,locator='Author abstract and displayed nn. 1, 5')],holds=['Microhistory affiliation held; full books, chapter and retrospective unread.'])

p='thomas_laqueur';s='tq233_laqueur_holmes2019'
P(p,
'A cultural historian of bodies, sexuality and death. Making Sex advances a historical account of the categories through which bodily difference was understood; the argument is controversial. Brooke Holmes challenges its division between premodern and modern bodies and, discussing Park, Nye, Cadden and King, argues that the evidence supports more varied and coexisting models. She also traces the book’s narrative of rupture to Foucault’s history of sexuality. That is a named scholarly interpretation of Laqueur, not a newly verified reading of all those originals. In his Berkeley interview, Laqueur explains another inquiry: the care and naming of the dead help construct enduring social relations. Making Sex, Solitary Sex and The Work of the Dead thus address historical meanings of embodied life while remaining distinct projects; the contested sex model is not adopted as a settled chronology.',
'Selected primary interview answers plus Holmes’s substantive 2019 article, opening argument and later passage beginning The rupture with the ancients, read. Critics’ originals, Laqueur’s 2003 reply and full books unread. The publisher reply page denied access and was not bypassed. Holmes’s report of criticism and intellectual resources remains explicitly mediated. No queer-history affiliation follows from topic alone.',
sources=[S(s,'Brooke Holmes, “Let Go of Laqueur: Towards New Histories of the Sexed Body,” Eugesta 9 (2019).','https://doi.org/10.54563/eugesta.356','Read opening body from For nearly thirty years through the critique of binaries, and later paragraphs The rupture with the ancients through queer-unhistoricist debate; notes 4–7, 60, 65–66. Named scholarly criticism, not the originals cited. Full article not claimed read.')],
edges=[E(p,'foucault','foucault',p,'Holmes traces the narrative of epochal rupture in Making Sex to Foucault’s history of sexuality.',s,'Holmes, paragraph beginning The rupture with the ancients and n65. Named reader’s specific intellectual connection, not personal transmission or complete agreement; originals unread.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Opening body argument; later Foucault/rupture paragraphs; nn. 4–7, 60, 65–66',access='Journal’s authorized open text')],holds=['Holmes, Park/Nye, Cadden and King have no individual endpoints here; criticism stays in the biography and source note. Original criticisms and Laqueur reply unread.'])
